@startuml fault-signal
' What happens when the processor faults, before and after.
' Grounded in Src/fault.c, Inc/fault.h, Startup/startup_stm32l476rgtx.s
' (the weak aliases) and tests/fault_test.c.

title A fault that says something, instead of going quiet

skinparam shadowing false

rectangle "Before" as B #ffdddd {
  card "any fault" as Bf
  card "vector table" as Bv
  card "Default_Handler:\n  b Infinite_Loop" as Bd
  card "board stops.\nLED unchanged, serial silent." as Bs

  Bf --> Bv
  Bv --> Bd
  Bd --> Bs
}

rectangle "After" as A #ddffdd {
  card "any fault" as Af
  card "vector table" as Av
  card "HardFault_Handler\nMemManage_Handler\nBusFault_Handler\nUsageFault_Handler" as Ah
  card "fault_trap(kind)" as At
  card "LED blinks a COUNT, forever" as Ab

  Af --> Av
  Av --> Ah
  Ah --> At
  At --> Ab
}

note bottom of Bs
  Indistinguishable from a working
  board that happens to be idle.
  You find out only because the
  button stopped responding.
end note

note bottom of Ab
  2 blinks  hard fault
  3 blinks  memory management
  4 blinks  bus fault
  5 blinks  usage fault

  Counted from across the room,
  with no debugger attached.
end note

note as How
  **How the override works**

  The startup file declares each handler
  .weak and aliases it to Default_Handler.
  Defining the same symbols in fault.c
  replaces those aliases at link time.

  The vendor-generated startup file is
  never edited. Verified by dumping the
  linked vector table: offset 0x0C holds
  0x080004c1, which is HardFault_Handler,
  not Default_Handler.
end note

note as Safe
  **Why a nop loop and not the millisecond clock**

  A fault handler must not depend on SysTick
  still running, on interrupts, or on any
  state the fault may have corrupted.

  board_init runs again first: if the fault
  hit before main got that far, the GPIO clock
  would still be gated off and the LED would
  stay dark, which is the exact silence this
  file exists to remove.
end note

note as Tested
  **What a host test can and cannot check**

  Cannot: that the LED lights, or that the
  vector table is right. Those are answered
  by the board and by objdump.

  Can: that four faults map to four DIFFERENT
  counts, each between 2 and 9. Two faults
  blinking the same number carry no more
  information than a dead board.
end note

@enduml
